WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.555
A factory camera inspects every part.

00:00:03.575 --> 00:00:06.805
Its detector catches ninety percent of real
defects,

00:00:06.825 --> 00:00:10.918
and raises a false alarm on only five percent
of good parts.

00:00:10.938 --> 00:00:12.142
Then it beeps.

00:00:12.162 --> 00:00:15.350
How likely is it that this part is really
defective?

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.568
Let's count instead of guessing.

00:00:17.588 --> 00:00:19.455
Picture a thousand parts.

00:00:19.475 --> 00:00:21.918
Defects are rare, just two percent:

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:25.250
twenty orange dots, among nine hundred eighty
good ones.

00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:27.055
Now run the detector.

00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:30.605
It flags eighteen of the twenty defects, and
misses two.

00:00:30.625 --> 00:00:35.525
But it also beeps on five percent of the good
parts: forty nine false alarms.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.040
So a part beeps.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:38.928
What is the chance it's really defective?

00:00:38.948 --> 00:00:40.585
Pause and decide.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:47.393
Keep only the parts that beeped, and gather
them: sixty seven alerts.

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:49.168
How many are real defects?

00:00:49.188 --> 00:00:50.493
Just eighteen.

00:00:50.513 --> 00:00:54.000
Eighteen out of sixty seven is about twenty
seven percent.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.540
If you guessed ninety, you answered a
different question.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.965
Ninety percent is the chance of a beep, given
a defect.

00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:03.827
We wanted the chance of a defect, given a
beep.

00:01:03.848 --> 00:01:08.260
Good parts are so common that their false
alarms swamp the real ones.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.248
Bayes' theorem is the same count, in
probabilities.

00:01:12.268 --> 00:01:16.485
The chance of a defect given a beep equals the
chance of a beep given a defect,

00:01:16.505 --> 00:01:19.673
times the base rate, over the chance of any
beep.

00:01:19.693 --> 00:01:22.185
That's point nine times point zero two,

00:01:22.205 --> 00:01:26.780
over sixty seven in a thousand: eighteen over
sixty seven again.

00:01:27.245 --> 00:01:30.925
Now make defects ten times as common: twenty
percent.

00:01:30.945 --> 00:01:32.875
The detector hasn't changed.

00:01:32.895 --> 00:01:37.012
But now one hundred eighty true alerts face
only forty false ones,

00:01:37.032 --> 00:01:41.170
so a beep means a real defect about eighty two
percent of the time.

00:01:41.695 --> 00:01:44.425
Back at two percent, these numbers have names.

00:01:44.445 --> 00:01:47.388
Recall: of the real defects, how many beeped?

00:01:47.408 --> 00:01:48.700
Eighteen of twenty.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.413
Precision: of the beeps, how many were real?

00:01:51.432 --> 00:01:53.245
Eighteen of sixty seven.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.672
Rare classes look just like this.

00:01:55.692 --> 00:01:59.810
In this photo, the camera fills twenty of a
thousand patches.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.385
And a model's confidence only means what it
says

00:02:02.405 --> 00:02:05.605
when it's calibrated at the base rate where
it's deployed.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.842
So remember three things.

00:02:07.862 --> 00:02:09.367
Start from the base rate.

00:02:09.387 --> 00:02:11.480
Count the true and false alerts.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.175
And the posterior is true alerts divided by
all alerts.

